    "summary": "Saint Stephen encore shines; acoustic gems and tight rock jams.",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a high‑energy first set with Black Peter, New Speedway Boogie and a soaring Saint Stephen encore. The second set balances mellow acoustic moments like I Know You Rider and Easy Wind with powerful rock numbers such as Hard To Handle and It's A Man's World.",
    "review": "On June 5, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead rolled out a setlist that swings from gentle folk‑rock to raw, electric fire. The acoustic opening—highlighted by crystal‑clear renditions of I Know You Rider and Easy Wind—sets a warm tone before the band launches into punchy numbers like Black Peter, New Speedway Boogie and Hard To Handle, keeping the momentum high throughout. A standout moment arrives in the second set when the group dives deep into It's A Man's World, then caps the night with an explosive Saint Stephen → Casey Jones encore that leaves the audience buzzing. Across all available sources the performance feels tight, the vocal harmonies shine, and even the occasional technical hiccup can’t dull the show’s overall brilliance.\n\nWhile several recordings capture the set in varying fidelity, the matrix tape from Dusborne (gd1970-06-05.123702.mtx.dusborne.flac16) offers the clearest window into this night—especially the vocal interplay on I Know You Rider and the balanced mix of acoustic and electric sections. Listeners seeking the definitive listening experience should start there, but any of the listed sources provide a worthwhile glimpse of a classic early‑70s Dead show.",
